<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kosala</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 460 BCE (approx.) · capital Shravasti</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A great kingdom of the middle Ganges plain, ruled from Shravasti, where the Buddha spent much of his teaching life before Magadha swallowed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kosala appears in Later Vedic texts as a kingdom east of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/panchala">Panchala</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gomti-river">Gomti</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarayu-river">Sarayu</a> rivers, with early centres at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saketa">Saketa</a>. Its kings claimed descent from the solar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikshvaku">Ikshvaku</a> line, the lineage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>. By the 7th and 6th centuries BCE it had become one of the strongest of the sixteen mahajanapadas, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravasti">Shravasti</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>King Mahakosala annexed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kashi">Kingdom of Kashi</a> and held the Shakya and Koliya republics as dependants. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasenajit">Prasenajit</a> was a lay follower of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Buddha</a>, and the merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anathapindika">Anathapindika</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jetavana">Jetavana</a> grove at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravasti">Shravasti</a>, where the Buddha spent many rainy seasons. Shravasti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saketa">Saketa</a> were wealthy trading cities on the northern route. Kosala's rivalry with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Magadha</a> was eased for a time by royal marriages.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasenajit">Prasenajit</a> was overthrown by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidudabha">Vidudabha</a>, who destroyed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shakya-republic">Shakyas</a> around the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Buddha</a>'s death. Soon after, Kosala was absorbed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a> or his successors in the 5th century BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya's</a> place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> kept the name Kosala alive in Indian tradition.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 700 BCE – 460 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:45.77%;width:4.78%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 460 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shravasti</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mahakosala, Prasenajit, Vidudabha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion, Buddhism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kosala, Koshala Mahajanapada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-580&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kosala" aria-label="Kingdom of Kosala on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc"